{# BIND-format TSIG keyfile for acme.sh's dns_nsupdate plugin.

   Rendered 0600 root:root by playbooks/ai-hosts.yml under the `acme` tag, with
   no_log set on the task so neither the content nor a --diff ever reaches a
   terminal or a log. The plugin passes this path to `nsupdate -k`; nsupdate
   parses the stanza below and takes BOTH the key name and the algorithm from it,
   which is why neither is a command-line flag anywhere in ACME_ISSUE_COMMAND.

   CONSUMER-NEUTRAL BY NAME AND BY CONTENT. The key this renders is not minted for
   this host — it is the astropath key the estate's ACME clients share, and its
   identity, algorithm and secret all arrive as variables from the Internal
   inventory. Nothing about which consumer is rendering it belongs in this file.

   Three things are load-bearing and none of them is cosmetic:

     * The key name must match the name astropath holds in its keyring, byte for
       byte, TRAILING DOT INCLUDED. nsupdate parses it as a domain name and sends
       it absolute on the wire; astropath compares it and answers BADKEY on any
       mismatch, or NOTAUTH when the name is one it has never been told about.
       The value comes from ACME_TSIG_KEY_NAME.

     * The algorithm must match what astropath registered against that key —
       hmac-sha256 for every current consumer. nsupdate's historical default is
       hmac-md5, which astropath refuses. The value comes from
       ACME_TSIG_KEY_ALGORITHM.

     * The secret is the single-base64 string astropath issued, reproduced
       verbatim. Decoding it, re-encoding it, or "fixing" its padding yields
       BADSIG on every UPDATE — a challenge that never lands rather than a clean
       authentication failure.

   No ansible_managed header: this file is a credential, and a rendered-by banner
   only adds a line to something that should stay minimal and unreadable.
-#}
key "{{ ACME_TSIG_KEY_NAME }}" {
	algorithm {{ ACME_TSIG_KEY_ALGORITHM }};
	secret "{{ ACME_NSUPDATE_TSIG_SECRET }}";
};
